 Branch execution is synchronous and uses an independent copy of the source.
 
+## Choose catalog identities and a race strategy
+
+Use `sim_catalog({"kind":"trainees"})` to read the simulator's actual trainee
+records before starting a career. The required kind is one of `scenarios`,
+`trainees`, `supports`, `factors`, or `skills`. The result is the native catalog
+response, including its full `items` list. It does not start a career or change
+live sessions. Use the returned trainee name, scenario id, support ids and factor
+ids with existing `sim_start` arguments. No identity is inferred by this wrapper;
+catalog contents depend on the native server's data root.
+
+To try a race preference independently, fork an existing career, then select a
+style on that named fork:
+
+```json
+{ "name": "sim_session_fork", "arguments": { "session": "study-42", "id": "try-front" } }
+{ "name": "sim_style", "arguments": { "session": "try-front", "style": "front" } }
+{ "name": "sim_state", "arguments": { "session": "try-front" } }
+```
+
+The required style is `front`, `pace`, `late`, `end`, or `auto`. Auto clears the
+explicit preference and restores the engine's best-aptitude selection. Changing
+style returns the native snapshot without spending a turn or advancing RNG.
+Existing sibling careers and saved checkpoints remain unchanged. A chosen style
+is a preference for the native race model, not a promise of a better result.
+
+Supply a nonempty `session` to target a named career explicitly. Omission uses
+the active career; `session: ""` is refused like other named run operations.
+A missing career remains a native error. Catalog and style calls use the same
+configured API and error handling as existing tools; neither is retried automatically.
+
 ## Example: save, fork, play, and compare
 
 These are `tools/call` parameter objects. Read returned ids instead of guessing
